1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,138
Welcome to lesson ten: storage.

2
00:00:03,238 --> 00:00:10,944
You'll keep data when containers restart, share files between containers, and give apps persistent disks that survive Pod deletion.

3
00:00:12,294 --> 00:00:18,800
Each container starts with a fresh copy of its image's files. Anything written there is lost when the container restarts.

4
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:27,102
Stateless apps like web servers don't care. But anything stateful, such as databases, uploads or queues, needs a volume.

5
00:00:28,452 --> 00:00:30,670
Volumes differ by how long they live.

6
00:00:30,770 --> 00:00:37,580
An emptyDir lives as long as the Pod. It survives container restarts, and two containers in a Pod can share it.

7
00:00:37,680 --> 00:00:47,338
hostPath uses a folder on the node. Avoid it for apps: the data is tied to one node, and mounting sensitive paths can give a container control of the whole node.

8
00:00:47,438 --> 00:00:51,128
For real persistent data, use a persistent volume claim.

9
00:00:52,478 --> 00:00:55,768
Kubernetes separates asking for storage from providing it.

10
00:00:55,868 --> 00:01:09,686
A persistent volume claim is like an order form: I need ten gigabytes of a fast type. A persistent volume is the actual disk. A storage class is the catalog of disk types, and a CSI driver is the supplier that creates the disks.

11
00:01:09,786 --> 00:01:17,268
Normally this is dynamic. You create a claim, the driver creates the disk and the volume automatically, and the two are bound together.

12
00:01:18,618 --> 00:01:22,500
Here's a claim for ten gigabytes, and a Pod using it by name.

13
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:29,218
Claims are namespaced, so the Pod must be in the same namespace. Volumes and storage classes are cluster-wide.

14
00:01:30,568 --> 00:01:33,554
Access modes say how a volume can be shared.

15
00:01:33,654 --> 00:01:42,352
Read write once means one node, not one Pod. Two Pods on the same node can share it. Use read write once Pod when you truly need a single writer.

16
00:01:42,452 --> 00:01:54,814
Block storage, like AWS EBS, only supports single-node access. Asking for read write many on it leaves the claim pending forever. For shared files, use file storage like EFS or NFS.

17
00:01:56,164 --> 00:01:59,390
A storage class has three settings that matter a lot.

18
00:01:59,490 --> 00:02:07,372
Reclaim policy Delete, the default, means deleting the claim deletes the disk and all its data. Use Retain for anything you can't lose.

19
00:02:07,472 --> 00:02:15,370
Wait for first consumer creates the disk only when a Pod is scheduled, in the same zone as that Pod. That avoids disks stuck in the wrong zone.

20
00:02:15,470 --> 00:02:18,568
And allow volume expansion lets you grow a claim later.

21
00:02:19,918 --> 00:02:22,552
A claim is pending until it's bound to a volume.

22
00:02:22,652 --> 00:02:29,910
When a claim with Retain is deleted, its volume becomes Released. The data is still there, but it isn't reused automatically.

23
00:02:30,010 --> 00:02:35,012
A claim that's in use by a Pod is protected: deleting it waits until the Pod is gone.

24
00:02:35,112 --> 00:02:41,874
Snapshots are useful, but they aren't a complete backup strategy. Tools like Velero back up both objects and data.

25
00:02:43,224 --> 00:02:48,594
Here's a common trap: a Deployment with three replicas, all using the same single-node disk.

26
00:02:48,694 --> 00:02:58,320
Pods on other nodes fail with a multi-attach error. Even one replica can hit this during a rolling update. And several database processes writing to one disk can corrupt it.

27
00:02:58,420 --> 00:03:08,014
Use one replica with the Recreate strategy, or a StatefulSet, which gives each Pod its own disk. For production databases, consider a managed service.

28
00:03:09,364 --> 00:03:15,278
In the lab, you'll lose a file on restart, then share files between two containers with emptyDir.

29
00:03:15,378 --> 00:03:21,148
You'll create a claim, write data, delete the Pod, and see the data survive in a new Pod.

30
00:03:21,248 --> 00:03:29,562
And you'll watch the Delete and Retain policies in action. If a non-root app can't write to a fresh volume, set fs group in the security context.

31
00:03:30,912 --> 00:03:32,106
Let's recap.

32
00:03:32,206 --> 00:03:37,544
Container files and emptyDir are temporary, and hostPath is for system agents only.

33
00:03:37,644 --> 00:03:42,982
A claim requests storage, and a storage class with a CSI driver creates the disk.

34
00:03:43,082 --> 00:03:49,316
Read write once means one node. The Delete policy destroys data, so use Retain where it matters.

35
00:03:49,416 --> 00:03:56,418
Next, you'll meet StatefulSets, which give each Pod its own disk, plus DaemonSets, Jobs and CronJobs.
